<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Sacred · Celebration · c. 1636 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Now Thank We All Our God</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="de">Nun danket alle Gott</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A German hymn of thanks written around 1636 by a pastor who lived through war, plague and famine; it is also sung in English.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Martin Rinkart; English translation by Catherine Winkworth</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Johann Crüger (first published it in 1647; sometimes attributed to Rinkart)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>German Lutherans</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>German, English</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Eilenburg, Saxony, Germany</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>c. 1636; tune published 1647</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Sacred · Celebration</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Martin Rinkart (printed 1636); English translation by Catherine Winkworth (19th century))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;Nun danket alle Gott&quot; was written around 1636 by Martin Rinkart, a Lutheran pastor who came to Eilenburg in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> at the beginning of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>. The walled town became a refuge for political and military fugitives, and the overcrowding brought deadly pestilence and famine; armies overran it three times. Rinkart's home sheltered the victims, though he was often hard-pressed to feed his own family. In the severe plague of 1637 he was the only pastor left alive in Eilenburg, burying as many as 50 people in a day and more than 4,000 in that year, his own wife among them.</p><p>A prolific writer of hymns, Rinkart printed &quot;Nun danket alle Gott&quot; in his Jesu Hertz-Büchlein (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a>, 1636) under the title &quot;Tisch-Gebetlein&quot;, a short prayer before meals. Its exact date is debated, but it was widely sung by the time the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a> was signed in 1648. Johann Crüger published it with its tune (Zahn No. 5142) in the 1647 edition of his Praxis pietatis melica; the melody is sometimes attributed to Rinkart but usually considered Crüger's. In English it is sung as &quot;Now thank we all our God&quot;, in the 19th-century translation by Catherine Winkworth, mostly to a modified form of the melody.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-sebastian-bach">Johann Sebastian Bach</a> used the hymn in his cantatas, among them BWV 79 and BWV 192, harmonised it for four voices and set it as a chorale prelude, BWV 657, among the Great Eighteen. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/felix-mendelssohn">Felix Mendelssohn</a> made the harmonisation now in standard use in 1840, when he took the hymn, in G major and with its German words, as the chorale of his Lobgesang (Hymn of Praise, Symphony No. 2). Max Reger wrote a chorale prelude on it in 1902, Sigfrid Karg-Elert used it in his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marche">Marche</a> Triomphale, John Rutter set it for choir and brass in 1974 and Václav Nelhýbel made a concertato for brass and organ in 1977.</p><p>A story holds that after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-leuthen">Battle of Leuthen</a> in 1757 the whole assembled Prussian army took up the hymn, and from it the melody is sometimes called the Leuthen Chorale. Historians and musicologists question the story and identify it as a later invention of Prussian propaganda.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A hymn of thanks to God, first printed as a short prayer before meals by a pastor of a town overrun by armies during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>. It is joyful and steady in mood.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">German</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">German</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Nun dancket alle Gott</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Now thank we all our God,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Mit Hertzen Mund vnd Händen</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>With heart, and hands, and voices,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Der grosse Dinge thut</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Who wondrous things hath done,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">An vns vnd aller Enden</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>In Whom His world rejoices;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Der vns von Mutter Leib</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Who from our mother's arms</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Vnd Kindes Beinen an</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Hath bless'd us on our way</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Vnzehlig viel zu gut</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>With countless gifts of love,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Vnd noch j[e]tzund gethan.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And still is ours to-day.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Der ewig reiche Gott</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>O may this bounteous God</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Woll vns auff vnser Leben</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Through all our life be near us,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Ein jmmer frölich Hertz</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>With ever joyful hearts</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Vnd edlen Frieden geben:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And blessèd peace to cheer us:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Vnd vns in seiner Gnad</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And keep us in His grace,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Erhalten fort vnd fort</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And guide us when perplex'd,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Vnd uns aus aller Noth</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And free us from all ills</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Erlösen hier vnd dort.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>In this world and the next.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Lob / Ehr v[n]d Preis sey Gott</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>All praise and thanks to God</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Dem Vater vnd dem Sohne</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The Father now be given,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Vnd dem der beyden gleich</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The Son, and Him Who reigns</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Im höchsten Himmels Throne:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>With Them in highest Heaven,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Dem dreymal einen Gott</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The One Eternal God,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Als Er vrsprünglich war</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Whom earth and Heav'n adore,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Vnd ist / vnd bleiben wird</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>For thus it was, is now,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Jetzund vnd jmmerdar.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And shall be evermore.Amen.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Martin Rinckart, Jesu Hertz-Büchlein (1636), original spelling; as quoted in Wikipedia's article (German Wikipedia). Text from <a href="https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nun_danket_alle_Gott" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p><p>English: tr. Catherine Winkworth, as in Hymns for the Coronation of His Majesty King Edward VII (1902). Text from <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Hymns_for_the_Coronation_of_His_Majesty_King_Edward_VII%2FNow_Thank_we_all_our_God" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="H44Bq7oWhGQ" data-title="Now Thank We All Our God: the Rundfunkchor Berlin, under Simon Halsey, sing Crüger's chorale in German (EG 321)"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">the Rundfunkchor Berlin, under Simon Halsey, sing Crüger's chorale in German (EG 321)</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel Rundfunkchor Berlin - Topic. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=H44Bq7oWhGQ" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Nun danket alle Gott, BWV 79 and BWV 192 (Johann Sebastian Bach)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">used in cantatas</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Nun danket alle Gott, BWV 657 (Johann Sebastian Bach)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">chorale prelude, one of the Great Eighteen</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Lobgesang, Symphony No. 2 (Felix Mendelssohn)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1840</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the hymn as its chorale, in the now-standard harmonisation</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">52 Chorale Preludes, Op. 67 No. 27 (Max Reger)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1902</span><span class="sg-chain-h">chorale prelude</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Marche Triomphale (Sigfrid Karg-Elert)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">used in an organ march</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Now thank we all our God (John Rutter)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1974</span><span class="sg-chain-h">setting for choir and brass</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Now Thank We All Our God: Concertato (Václav Nelhýbel)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1977</span><span class="sg-chain-h">setting for brass, organ and congregation</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Now thank we all our God (Catherine Winkworth)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">translation (19th century)</span></li></ul></section>
